 delays to our unlocking  that we're debating this even were not  inevitable they were in fact totally  avoidable  the british public have been magnificent  throughout this crisis they followed the  rules  and they played their part but when they  see world leaders ignoring social  distancing at a barbecue but are told  the rules  cannot yet be relaxed for them when they  see thousands of people attending  football matches  but they're told they cannot attend  their own children's school sports day  they grow frustrated at what they see is  a lack of consistency from those who  make the rules  but that frustration grows into anger  when they see a prime minister who has  thrown it all away  by keeping the borders open and letting  the delta variant run wild  in the country as a result the delta  variant is now making up 96  of new infections that didn't happen by  accident and as the chief medical  officer said on monday  we would be lifting restrictions now if  it were not for the delta variant  all that good work all the benefits of  the vaccine blown because the prime  minister  was once again too slow just as he was  too slow with the first lockdown the  second lockdown  and the third i know the government will  say that they acted as soon as they  could on the information they had  but the explanation why they didn't act  soon on the delta variant has actually  changed in the last few days  we were initially told on multiple  occasions that the data did not support  putting india on the red list earlier  because positivity rates of the new  variant were three times higher to  pakistan  but now we are told the reason why it  wasn't put on earlier is because the  variance had not been identified  as one of interest or concern neither  explanation  stands up to scrutiny the only published  data on the indian variant does not show  a positivity rate three times as high  for pakistan  and the idea that action was taken  shortly before it was designated as a  variant